  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">When things go wrong</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Designed for the bad day, <em>not</em> the demo.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Authorization policy and service availability are separate questions. You choose per tier what happens when Clearance is unreachable; the defaults let tiers 0 and 1 through and hold tiers 2 and 3. A second knob covers the quieter failure: if evidence can't reach a witnessed checkpoint, tier 3 waits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cmp table-wrap" data-reveal>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>What happens</th><th>Containment</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Clearance is down</td><td>Tiers 2 and 3 hold; tiers 0 and 1 proceed per your outage policy</td><td>Degraded-mode records written on recovery; alert</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Clearance is compromised</td><td>An attacker could issue tokens</td><td>Grants bind to rendered objects the attacker must also land; short-lived signing identity; two humans on tier 3; issuance shows in the ledger</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The GitHub App token leaks</td><td>Checks and comments could be forged</td><td>Minimal App permissions; checks are advisory, admission is the gate</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An approver account is compromised</td><td>A fraudulent tier 2 clearance</td><td>Approver can't be the initiator; tier 3 needs two; IdP MFA; the subject is in the ledger</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A database admin rewrites history</td><td>Records changed or deleted</td><td>The ledger holds hashes, and witnesses refuse a shorter or rewritten history; <code>clearance verify</code> names the altered line</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A witness is unreachable</td><td>Records keep being written; no new checkpoint</td><td>Each record carries its assurance state; the packet reports the window; tier 3 waits by default</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A sync skips the hook (<code>--strategy apply</code>)</td><td>No wait shown in Argo</td><td>Admission still refuses any object no grant covers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Someone runs <code>kubectl apply</code></td><td>A change skips Argo entirely</td><td>Recorded as out-of-band, or as break-glass if they took that path; flip one field to deny it outright</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="cols" data-reveal>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Two entry points, one gate</p>
          <h2 class="h2" style="font-size:clamp(1.8rem,2.6vw + 0.6rem,2.6rem)">Generic first. <em>Native</em> when Autopilot lands.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>The generic entry point is a GitHub pull request landing through Argo CD. It's what every team that doesn't run Hangar uses, so it ships first.</p>
          <p>On a Hangar cluster an agent's work arrives as an Autopilot <code>AgentRun</code>, a Crossplane XR, and the same controller gates it at admission. The runtime tier decides what the run may do; the deployment tier decides what may land. A task id on every runtime record links the call that proposed a change to the clearance that landed it. <span class="chip proposal">Proposal</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Reference deployment</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Clearance clears its <em>own</em> releases.</h2>
        <p class="sub">The Hangar lab, a dev and a prod cluster, is the reference deployment and the demo. A clearance product that can't clear its own release isn't credible. Every phase closes with negative tests like these:</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="practices" data-reveal>
        <li><h3>Held, cleared, synced</h3><p>An agent pull request to prod is held, cleared by a human, and syncs. The same change to dev auto-clears in under a second.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Force-push refused</h3><p>A push after approval renders a tree no grant covers, and admission refuses it. A sync that skips the hook is refused the same way.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Config tiered by effect</h3><p>A ConfigMap-only change to prod is tiered by its rendered diff and clears with the image digest unchanged.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Side door caught</h3><p>A direct <code>kubectl apply</code> to prod appears as an out-of-band exception within the scan interval.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Outage behaves as configured</h3><p>With Clearance stopped, a dev change proceeds and a secret rotation holds. Both are recorded on recovery.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Verified from the outside</h3><p>A 30-day packet verifies with <code>clearance verify</code> on a machine that can't reach the recorder, against the checkpoints and witness countersignatures.</p></li>
        <li><h3>History can't shrink</h3><p>Truncate the ledger and re-sign a checkpoint: the witness refuses it. Alter one row: the verifier names the line.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

